// The number terminals, defined once.
//
// Two grammars need it -- `regimen` and `record` -- and for a while each
// spelled it out for itself. The two spellings admitted the same strings and
// a comment in one of them said so, which is the two-readers class at the
// terminal level: nothing executed the claim, so nothing would notice the day
// one of them was widened and the other was not. A regime is identified by
// the settings that name it, so a number one reader accepts and the other
// refuses is two regimes wearing one name.
//
// Included by both grammars through a second `#[grammar]` attribute rather
// than copied into them, so there is one text to change and no second place
// to forget.
//
// `-0` is not here, deliberately: it and `0` project to the same value, so two
// byte-different documents would name one regime. Narrowing is the permitted
// direction, and this is where the narrowing lives.
integer  = @{ ("-" ~ nonzero) | int_part }

// The parts travel with it. Sharing `integer` alone and leaving each grammar
// its own `int_part` would move the divergence rather than remove it -- the
// fractional rules are built out of these too.
int_part = _{ "0" | nonzero }
nonzero  = _{ ASCII_NONZERO_DIGIT ~ ASCII_DIGIT* }

// AND THE FRACTIONAL TERMINAL, for the same reason and by the same ruling --
// 2026-09-12. `regimen::float` and `record::decimal` were byte-identical
// modulo the rule name, and so were their negative halves, with not even a
// comment claiming they agreed. The `-0` ruling named the INTEGER terminal
// because that was the divergence in front of it; the rule was never
// integer-specific.
//
// Each grammar keeps its own NAME -- `float` there, `decimal` here -- as an
// ATOMIC alias of this rule. Atomic matters: `@` suppresses inner tokens, so
// `Rule::float` and `Rule::decimal` still carry the whole span and no child,
// exactly as they did when each spelled the digits out. The readers that
// match on those rules are untouched.
//
// AN EXACT DECIMAL, never a binary float: the digits as written. A binary
// float would read `0.6` back as `0.59999999999999998`, and a regime
// identified by its settings cannot have a value that changes when it is
// round-tripped.
//
// `-0.0` is refused. One spelling per value, and negative zero is a second
// spelling of zero -- the same narrowing `-0` gets above, and in the same
// direction: `regimen/fixtures/invalid/negative-zero.toml` and
// `negative-zero-float.toml` are the pair that refuse them, so neither half
// of the narrowing rests on this comment. (An earlier draft of this comment
// claimed `-0` was still accepted as an integer, as an asymmetry a later
// version should settle. It said that of the rule three lines above it, which
// refuses it. There is no asymmetry and there is nothing to settle.)
//
// No exponent. `7e-1` is a second spelling of `0.7`.
fraction = @{ ("-" ~ negative_fraction) | (int_part ~ "." ~ ASCII_DIGIT+) }

// `ASCII_NONZERO_DIGIT` in the second alternative is a RESTATEMENT, not the
// enforcement: `"0"*` is greedy and pest does not backtrack a repetition, so
// whatever follows it is already not a zero. It is spelled out because the
// rule is about which digit may be nonzero, and a reader who narrowed `"0"*`
// to `"0"?` with `ASCII_DIGIT` there would reopen the hole in silence.
negative_fraction = _{
    (nonzero ~ "." ~ ASCII_DIGIT+) | ("0" ~ "." ~ "0"* ~ ASCII_NONZERO_DIGIT ~ ASCII_DIGIT*)
}
